From: Thorch contributors
Subject: [PATCH] input: qcom-hv-haptics: stop force-toggling HAPTICS_EN when the brake poll times out

haptics_wait_brake_complete() polls HAP_CFG_HPWR_INTF_REG for
HPWR_DISABLED after a bounded play stops, and if the poll runs out of
its budget it falls back to haptics_toggle_module_enable():

	if (timeout == 0) {
		dev_warn(chip->dev, "poll HPWR_DISABLED failed after stopped play\n");
		return haptics_toggle_module_enable(chip);
	}

On the Thor this fallback fires on most taps, and the power cycle it
performs is itself audible. With dynamic debug on the haptics driver a
burst of taps logs:

	polling HPWR_INTF timeout 6, value = 3
	polling HPWR_INTF timeout 5, value = 3
	...
	polling HPWR_INTF timeout 1, value = 3
	poll HPWR_DISABLED failed after stopped play
	haptics module disabled
	haptics module enabled

Every poll reads 3, which is HPWR_READY, and never 0, which is
HPWR_DISABLED. This board is HAP525_HV with hbst_revision 0x2, and
haptics_config_wa() only sets SW_CTRL_HBST for HAP_BOOST_V0P1
(0x1), so the driver never software-controls the hBoost rail here.
hBoost holds the rail up, HPWR_READY is the steady powered state
rather than a fault or a completion signal, and the poll's success
condition is not reachable while hBoost owns the rail.

A minority of stops do log "stopped play completely", so the rail does
fall to HPWR_DISABLED sometimes, but only in a racy window against the
computed brake budget. That makes the fallback fire on some taps and
not others, which is what the alternating vibration strength is: each
toggle drops HAPTICS_EN and votes hBoost down and back up, and the
taps that happen to catch the HPWR_DISABLED edge skip that transition.

Note that haptics_check_hpwr_status() in the same driver already
accepts both HPWR_DISABLED and HPWR_READY as settled, which is why
Vmax programming does not misbehave here while this poll does. Fixing
that by also accepting HPWR_READY here would be wrong: it would break
out on the first poll roughly 2.4 ms into every stop and truncate the
brake pattern rather than wait for it.

Keep the recovery toggle only for states that are neither HPWR_DISABLED
nor HPWR_READY, which is what a genuinely wedged block looks like, and
drop the warning for the powered-idle case since it is the expected
outcome on an hBoost rail rather than an error.
---
 drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
@@ -1722,10 +1722,11 @@ static int haptics_wait_brake_complete(struct haptics_chip *chip)
 		dev_dbg(chip->dev, "polling HPWR_INTF timeout %d, value = %d\n",
 				timeout, val);
 	} while (--timeout);
 
 	if (timeout == 0) {
-		dev_warn(chip->dev, "poll HPWR_DISABLED failed after stopped play\n");
-		return haptics_toggle_module_enable(chip);
+		dev_dbg(chip->dev, "HPWR_INTF still %d after stopped play, not toggling\n", val);
+		if ((val & HPWR_INTF_STATUS_MASK) != HPWR_READY)
+			return haptics_toggle_module_enable(chip);
 	}
 
 	return 0;
